Defend continuing service through observation and change (@wu-operations-review: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @water-and-sanitation, #source "../sources/Asset-Management-for-Water-Systems-EPA.lingua#r_78ZKEFSZ16WYQ5E62DWKR8FP3N", #source "../sources/Effective-Utility-Management-EPA-USDA.lingua#r_5W9CSGM4NJKGFDES86XNMQFBRD", #source "../sources/Water-Safety-Plan-Manual-WHO.lingua#r_3JMZKS3FG80FKX9YRQPEJMA4VD", #source "../sources/Sanitation-Safety-Planning-WHO.lingua#r_2FF0R519TGR5RNY3EVV7APPZ86", #water-and-sanitation "../research/A-whole-water-service-is-the-subject-of-the-investigation.lingua#r_5QD5W19FRQ40Y1AWERAP6HNYJT") { r_17C0PJ3R16GNKATNSRSJH9JGS2
**Read:** Complete the relevant operating manuals, [Asset management for small water systems — US EPA](../sources/Asset-Management-for-Water-Systems-EPA.lingua#r_78ZKEFSZ16WYQ5E62DWKR8FP3N), [Effective utility management — rural and small systems guidebook](../sources/Effective-Utility-Management-EPA-USDA.lingua#r_5W9CSGM4NJKGFDES86XNMQFBRD) and the chosen control study. Use [Water safety plan manual — WHO and IWA, second edition](../sources/Water-Safety-Plan-Manual-WHO.lingua#r_3JMZKS3FG80FKX9YRQPEJMA4VD) and [Sanitation safety planning — WHO, second edition](../sources/Sanitation-Safety-Planning-WHO.lingua#r_2FF0R519TGR5RNY3EVV7APPZ86) to revisit the consequences across the whole service.

Bring sensing, control, protection, acceptance, maintenance, staffing and recovery into the same chronological account. Start with a plain explanation of who receives what service. Then show the physical balances, actual observations, uncertainties and decision authority that support that account.

**Work:** Test normal operation, a changed demand, unavailable input, measurement failure and a prolonged interruption. Use the original storage example as an inspectable beginning, then a model detailed enough for the question you choose. Independently verify at least one calculation and explain where your model cannot predict the real consequence.

The advanced study should compare two feasible operating arrangements and show how new evidence changes a decision. Separate performed observation, computed result and proposed trial throughout. Continue into the whole-system studies to join this operating account with source, treatment, wastewater and industrial knowledge.

**Return:** [Water, sanitation and industrial utilities](../Water-sanitation-and-industrial-utilities.lingua#r_7B9GRCJFHVBEEQEXKWCR2QMMPN).
**Continue:** [A whole water service is the subject of the investigation](../research/A-whole-water-service-is-the-subject-of-the-investigation.lingua#r_5QD5W19FRQ40Y1AWERAP6HNYJT).

